            to be able to explain.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector →</strong> the child named <em>Sprite</em>, then <strong>Window →
            Animation → Animator</strong>: the two parameters from 4.2, <code>Direction</code> and
            the <code>Attack</code> trigger, are waiting for something that can say which behaviour
            is live.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Scene view →</strong> watch one full beat and mark the instant the enemy ought
            to switch behaviour. That instant is an arrow, and every arrow needs a condition written
            on it.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <p>The machine, as the player experiences it — and then the question of which of these rows your code actually implements:</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>From</th><th>Condition or event</th><th>To</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td>PATROL</td>
            <td>distance to the player is inside the aggro radius</td>
            <td>CHASE</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>CHASE</td>
            <td>every frame: move toward the player, normalized</td>
            <td>CHASE (stay)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>CHASE</td>
            <td>contact with the player, and the damage cooldown has elapsed</td>
            <td>ATTACK</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>ATTACK</td>
            <td>damage delivered, cooldown started</td>
            <td>CHASE</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>CHASE</td>
            <td>distance is outside the radius (or outside the give-up radius if you built one)</td>
            <td>PATROL</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p><strong>One row of that table is a lie.</strong> In your code ATTACK is not a state — it is a callback that fires when the two colliders meet, so it can fire mid-chase <em>or mid-patrol</em>. Drawing it as a state is still right, because that is how it feels to the player; just be able to say which rows your code implements and which it only feels like.</p>

    <p>There are three ways to hold those states, and they are priced rather than ranked — the question is never which is correct, but which cost you will pay for this enemy, this week:</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Representation</th><th>What it looks like in the file</th><th>What it buys you</th><th>What it costs you</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Boolean flags — one bool per behaviour</strong></td>
            <td><code>isChasing</code>, <code>isAttacking</code>, and one per behaviour after that.</td>
            <td>Cheap to add the first flag, and genuinely readable with exactly two behaviours. It is also the smallest change from what you have.</td>
            <td>Every flag doubles the number of combinations: four flags describe sixteen situations and most of them are nonsense. Nothing in the language stops you being "chasing and attacking and fleeing" at once — you get a bug instead of a compile error.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>An enum state variable</strong></td>
            <td>One enum listing the states, plus one field of that type, read by a <code>switch</code>.</td>
            <td>The set of legal states is written down in exactly one place, so illegal combinations cannot even be expressed. You can print the state name, drive the animator from it, and read every transition out of one <code>switch</code>.</td>
            <td>One field, one enum declaration and one transition rule — and you must decide <em>every</em> exit on purpose. The version you have gave you the exits for free; here the one you forget is a state the enemy can never leave.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>An enum, and one method per state</strong></td>
            <td>The same enum and field, with each case in the <code>switch</code> reduced to a single call: <code>case EnemyState.Chase: ChaseTick(distance); break;</code></td>
            <td>Each state's code becomes a named block you can read on its own, and the dispatch at the top of the frame reads as a table of contents.</td>
            <td>You have to decide what each state is handed — the distance? the target? — and the transition now sits one hop away from the selection that reads the field.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Naming the Machine</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Right now, is there any field in <code>EnemyController.cs</code> that holds what the enemy is currently doing? If not, what <em>is</em> holding it — and what does that cost you when you want to debug?</li>
<li>With two behaviours, a single bool is perfectly readable. How many combinations do four booleans describe, and how many of those are real states? What does that ratio tell you about scaling the flag approach?</li>
<li>Your attack is a callback, not a state. Can it fire while the enemy is patrolling? Walk into a patrolling enemy and find out — then say whether your diagram is a description of the code or a description of the player's experience.</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Architectural specification for your machine.</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>Which of the three representations did you choose, what type holds the state, and where does that field live — class body or method — with what first value?</li>
        <li>Where does each transition live, and what condition is written on the arrow back to PATROL? When the machine returns to PATROL, does the enemy resume its old beat or start a fresh one, and what does the player see either way?</li>
        <li>How do you print the current state's <em>name</em> so you can read it in the Console, and which animator parameter from <a href="4.2.html">4.2</a> can now be driven from the state directly rather than guessed from the movement?</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Break "the enemy should switch between patrolling, chasing and attacking" into its parts before you write any code, and name the earlier lesson you lifted each one from</li>
        <li>Draw the enemy's state machine from memory, with a condition written on every arrow</li>
        <li>Compare the flag-based and enum-based representations, and defend the one you chose</li>
        <li>Say where the return-to-patrol transition lives in your code, and what condition it tests</li>
        <li>Label sequencing, selection and iteration in real code</li>
        <li>Explain why your drawn ATTACK state and your coded attack are not quite the same thing</li>
        <li>Print the current state's name without a UI</li>
        <li>Commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

